  <div class="tag">Inventory Optimization</div>

  <h1>Safety Stock Formula<br><span>Why Your Current One Is Wrong</span></h1>

  <p class="subtitle">If your safety stock calculation only accounts for demand variability and ignores supplier reliability, you're probably overstocking or understocking -- often by a lot.</p>

  <div class="hook-stat">
    <p><strong>The reality:</strong> If your supplier lead time varies by +/- 5 days and you use a fixed 7-day lead time in your safety stock formula, your actual service level isn't 95% -- it's closer to 50%.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="formula-section">
    <div class="section-label">The Correct Formula</div>
    <div class="formula-box">
      <div class="formula-main">
        SS = Z x SQRT(LT_avg x sigma_demand^2 + demand_avg^2 x sigma_LT^2)
      </div>
      <div class="formula-vars">
        <div class="var-item"><code>Z</code> Service level factor (1.65 for 95%)</div>
        <div class="var-item"><code>LT_avg</code> Average lead time (days)</div>
        <div class="var-item"><code>sigma_demand</code> Demand variability (std dev)</div>
        <div class="var-item"><code>demand_avg</code> Average daily demand</div>
        <div class="var-item"><code>sigma_LT</code> Lead time variability (std dev)</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-label">What This Actually Means</div>
    <p style="font-size:14px; color:#b6c2d6; line-height:1.6; margin-bottom:20px;">
      You need to account for <strong style="color:#2dd4bf;">BOTH</strong> demand variability AND supplier reliability. Most small businesses only track one -- usually demand, because it's in their sales data. Supplier lead time variability comes from delivery records, which most don't systematically track.
    </p>

    <div class="comparison-row">
      <div class="approach-card approach-old">
        <div class="approach-label">Traditional Approach</div>
        <h3>Fixed Lead Time Only</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>SS = Z x sigma_demand x SQRT(LT)</li>
          <li>Assumes supplier is always on time</li>
          <li>Simple but dangerously wrong</li>
          <li>Service level drops to ~50%</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="approach-card approach-new">
        <div class="approach-label">Variable Lead Time</div>
        <h3>Dual-Variability Model</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Accounts for demand AND lead time variance</li>
          <li>True safety stock based on real data</li>
          <li>Slightly more complex, much more accurate</li>
          <li>Service level matches target (95%)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="example-box">
    <div class="example-title">Real Example: A D2C Skincare Brand</div>
    <div class="example-grid">
      <div class="example-item">
        <div class="val">Fixed LT</div>
        <div class="label">SS calculated with fixed 14-day lead time = <strong style="color:#ef4444;">820 units</strong></div>
      </div>
      <div class="example-item">
        <div class="val">Variable LT</div>
        <div class="label">SS with actual lead time variance = <strong style="color:#2dd4bf;">1,240 units</strong></div>
      </div>
      <div class="example-item">
        <div class="val">+51%</div>
        <div class="label">The gap. Under fixed LT, they were 51% understocked and losing sales every month.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <p><strong>The takeaway:</strong> Enter your lead times (or let us track them), and the system calculates true safety stock that accounts for how reliable your suppliers actually are. At Verit Analytics, this is built into our inventory optimization engine -- per SKU, automatically.</p>
